    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-11022",
      "problem": "Sketch the domain of $g(x,y)=\\dfrac{1}{\\ln(x+y)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14246566/assets/4a8d251b82227d9a0eed9bc1cd085f9c52ad698f3f5eee0c3d73be47b455ac66.svg)\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD = \\left \\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\, : \\, y \\gt -x, \\, y \\neq 1-x \\right \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we plot the corresponding domain. \n\n- Starting with $y> -x,$ we draw a dashed line $y=-x$ and shade the region *above* the line.\n\n- Then, we draw a dashed line $y=1-x$ through the shaded region.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14246566/assets/6aa1aaeadb177cf5a80bcb12a247725cb3396586e7516c3ba30b46b03a9d2612.png)",
